<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Buayan Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1905 CE (approx.) · capital Buayan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An upriver Maguindanao sultanate on the Pulangi River of Mindanao, long the rival of the coastal Maguindanao Sultanate.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Buayan was the upstream, sa raya, state of the Pulangi river basin in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>, while <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maguindanao-sultanate">Maguindanao</a> held the coast, sa ilud. Both traced their <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-kabungsuan">Sharif Kabungsuan</a>, a Johor-born preacher of the early 16th century. Buayan's rulers controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing interior and its links with highland peoples.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Buayan's datus governed through kinship networks and controlled trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, slaves and forest goods passing down the river. In the 19th century, as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maguindanao-sultanate">Maguindanao</a> weakened under Spanish pressure, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datu-uto">Datu Uto</a> of Buayan became the strongest leader in the Pulangi valley and acquired firearms through traders.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Spanish expeditions in the 1880s forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datu-uto">Datu Uto</a> to submit, though he kept much of his power. After 1899 the Americans took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mindanao">Mindanao</a>, and by 1905 Buayan's independence was ended. The area is now part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangsamoro">Bangsamoro</a> region.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/buayan-sultanate.jpg" alt="Buayan Sultanate" width="320" height="358"><figcaption>Image: Delirium333 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Buayan.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1500 CE – 1905 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Buayan</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Datu Uto</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Maguindanao</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/philippines">Philippines</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sultanate of Buayan, Buayan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1702&amp;focus=buayan-sultanate" aria-label="Buayan Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="8149 1950 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Buayan Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M8469 2206L8379 2176"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8379 2176h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8469 2206h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8469 2206h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Buayan. <a href="/atlas?year=1702&amp;focus=buayan-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
